.TH MKSCRIPT 1 "2026-08-21" "mkscript 1.5.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mkscript \- create starter files safely across Linux, macOS, and Windows
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mkscript
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-files\fR [\fIdepth\fR]]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-f\fR|\fB\-\-files\fR]
\fIname\fR
[\fIname\fR ...]
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-t\fR|\fB\-\-template\fR \fItemplate\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR|\fB\-\-strict\fR]
[\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-global\fR]
[\fB\-c\fR]
[\fB\-r\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.I path
.br
.B mkscript
[\fB\-g\fR|\fB\-\-global\fR]
\fB\-mv\fR
.I source
.I target
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mkscript
creates a new file at the requested path from a platform-native script template or from a Docker, Kubernetes, Terraform, or Ansible starter template.
On Linux and macOS, the default template is Bash.
On native Windows, the PowerShell implementation creates a .cmd file by default.
.PP
For Bash files on Linux and macOS,
.B mkscript
writes the standard shebang followed by a small comment header with the script name, a blank description line, the local creation date, and the creator login name:
.PP
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Script: hello-world
# Description:
# Created: YYYY-MM-DD
# Creator: login-user
.EE
.PP
For Windows command files,
.B mkscript
writes
.B @echo off
followed by the same metadata header rendered as
.B rem
comments.
.PP
For Dockerfile, Docker Compose, Kubernetes, Terraform, and Ansible files on every platform,
.B mkscript
writes the same metadata header with
.B File
in place of
.BR Script .
.PP
When
.B \-s
or
.B \-\-strict
is supplied,
.B mkscript
adds a platform-native strict-mode line:
.PP
.EX
Linux/macOS: set -euo pipefail
Windows:      setlocal EnableExtensions EnableDelayedExpansion
.EE
.PP
When
.B \-g
or
.B \-\-global
is supplied on Linux or macOS,
.B mkscript
creates a symlink in the detected user bin directory using the script basename.
If the requested path does not exist yet,
.B mkscript
creates the new Bash script and then creates the symlink.
If the requested path already exists locally,
.B mkscript
asks for confirmation and creates only the symlink without rewriting the local file.
.PP
When
.B \-g
or
.B \-\-global
is supplied on Windows,
.B mkscript
creates a managed .cmd wrapper in the user bin directory using the script basename.
If the requested path does not exist yet,
.B mkscript
creates the new .cmd file and then creates the wrapper.
If the requested path already exists locally,
.B mkscript
asks for confirmation and creates only the wrapper without rewriting the local file.
.PP
On Linux and macOS,
.B \-s ,
.BR \-\-strict ,
.BR \-g ,
.BR \-\-global ,
.BR \-c ,
and
.B \-r
are supported only for the Bash template.
On Windows, the default script template is
.BR cmd .
Windows does not support
.BR \-\-template\ bash .
.PP
The non-script templates supported on every platform are
.BR terraform ,
.BR ansible ,
.BR dockerfile ,
.BR docker-compose ,
.BR k8s-namespace ,
.BR k8s-pod ,
.BR k8s-deployment ,
.BR k8s-service ,
.BR k8s-configmap ,
.BR k8s-secret ,
.BR k8s-ingress ,
.BR k8s-networkpolicy ,
.BR k8s-serviceaccount ,
.BR k8s-role ,
.BR k8s-rolebinding ,
.BR k8s-clusterrole ,
.BR k8s-clusterrolebinding ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolume ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolumeclaim ,
.BR k8s-storageclass ,
.BR k8s-statefulset ,
.BR k8s-daemonset ,
.BR k8s-job ,
.BR k8s-cronjob ,
and
.BR k8s-horizontalpodautoscaler .
.PP
.B \-c
checks whether the expected global symlink or wrapper already exists and prints its path when it does.
.PP
.B \-r
removes the expected global symlink or wrapper after confirmation.
.PP
.B \-f
or
.B \-\-files
scans the current directory tree and prints a table of matching local files.
.I depth
may be supplied as a single digit from 0 through 9 to limit how many subfolder levels are scanned.
On Linux and macOS, a file is listed when it ends with
.BR .sh ,
is executable, or is the local target of a managed global symlink.
On Windows, a file is listed when it ends with
.BR .cmd ,
.BR .bat ,
.BR .ps1 ,
or
.BR .sh .
.PP
When
.B \-f
or
.B \-\-files
receives one to nine names from arguments or newline-separated standard input,
.B mkscript
switches to lookup mode and prints the resolved location and parent directory for each requested name.
Lookup names may be exact names or quoted shell-style glob patterns such as
.BR 'install-wifi*' .
Quote pattern arguments so the shell does not expand them before
.B mkscript
receives them.
Lookup mode checks an exact path first, then exact or pattern command matches on
.BR PATH ,
then current-tree and wider filename matches.
On Windows, command lookup uses native Windows command resolution.
Lookup mode returns the first resolved match for each query.
.PP
.B \-mv
moves an existing local Bash script to a new path on Linux/macOS or an existing local command script to a new path on Windows, and recreates the global shortcut when the source was linked already or when
.B \-g
is supplied.
On Linux and macOS,
.B \-mv
also preserves the source file permission mode.
.PP
Existing files, symlinks, directories, and managed wrapper paths are never overwritten.
.PP
On Linux,
.B mkscript
uses
.BR ~/.local/bin .
On macOS, it prefers a personal local bin directory already on
.B PATH
before falling back to
.BR ~/.local/bin .
On Windows, it uses
.BR %LOCALAPPDATA%\\mkscript\\bin
unless
.B MKSCRIPT_BIN_DIR
is set.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-template
Select the file template.
Supported values are
.BR bash ,
.BR dockerfile ,
.BR docker-compose ,
.BR k8s-namespace ,
.BR k8s-pod ,
.BR k8s-deployment ,
.BR k8s-service ,
.BR k8s-configmap ,
.BR k8s-secret ,
.BR k8s-ingress ,
.BR k8s-networkpolicy ,
.BR k8s-serviceaccount ,
.BR k8s-role ,
.BR k8s-rolebinding ,
.BR k8s-clusterrole ,
.BR k8s-clusterrolebinding ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolume ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolumeclaim ,
.BR k8s-storageclass ,
.BR k8s-statefulset ,
.BR k8s-daemonset ,
.BR k8s-job ,
.BR k8s-cronjob ,
.BR k8s-horizontalpodautoscaler ,
.BR terraform ,
and
.BR ansible
on Linux and macOS, and
.BR cmd ,
.BR dockerfile ,
.BR docker-compose ,
.BR k8s-namespace ,
.BR k8s-pod ,
.BR k8s-deployment ,
.BR k8s-service ,
.BR k8s-configmap ,
.BR k8s-secret ,
.BR k8s-ingress ,
.BR k8s-networkpolicy ,
.BR k8s-serviceaccount ,
.BR k8s-role ,
.BR k8s-rolebinding ,
.BR k8s-clusterrole ,
.BR k8s-clusterrolebinding ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolume ,
.BR k8s-persistentvolumeclaim ,
.BR k8s-storageclass ,
.BR k8s-statefulset ,
.BR k8s-daemonset ,
.BR k8s-job ,
.BR k8s-cronjob ,
.BR k8s-horizontalpodautoscaler ,
.BR terraform ,
and
.BR ansible
on Windows.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-files
List matching local files under the current directory tree.
On Linux and macOS, listing mode prints path, kind, executable state, and global-link state.
On Windows, listing mode prints path and kind.
When supplied, the optional
.I depth
value must be a single digit from 0 through 9, where 0 means the current directory only.
When one to nine
.I name
arguments are supplied, or when newline-separated names are piped to standard input, lookup mode prints a table with query, found state, result type, resolved location, and parent directory.
Quoted shell-style glob patterns such as
.BR 'install-wifi*'
are supported in lookup mode.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-strict
Add
.B set -euo pipefail
after the shebang for the Bash template on Linux/macOS, or
.B setlocal EnableExtensions EnableDelayedExpansion
for the
.B cmd
template on Windows.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-global
Create a global shortcut in the detected user bin directory.
On Linux and macOS this is a symlink for the Bash template.
On Windows this is a managed .cmd wrapper for the generated command file or for an existing local path after confirmation.
.TP
.B \-c
Check whether the expected global symlink or wrapper exists and print its path when it does.
.TP
.B \-r
Remove the expected global symlink or wrapper after confirmation.
.TP
.B \-mv
Move an existing local Bash script on Linux/macOS or an existing local command script on Windows, preserve the permission mode on Linux/macOS, and recreate the global shortcut or wrapper when appropriate.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Display usage information and exit successfully.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Display the program version and exit successfully.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 64
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 1
The requested link or wrapper was not present for check or removal mode, or one or more lookup names were not found.
.TP
.B 73
The target script could not be created safely.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Create a simple script in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript hello-world
.EE
.TP
Create a Terraform starter file:
.EX
mkscript --template terraform main.tf
.EE
.TP
Create an Ansible playbook starter file:
.EX
mkscript site.yml --template ansible
.EE
.TP
Create a Dockerfile starter:
.EX
mkscript --template dockerfile Dockerfile
.EE
.TP
Create a Docker Compose starter:
.EX
mkscript --template docker-compose compose.yaml
.EE
.TP
Create a Kubernetes Deployment starter:
.EX
mkscript --template k8s-deployment deployment.yaml
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script:
.EX
mkscript --strict deploy
.EE
.TP
Create a strict-mode script with a global shortcut or wrapper:
.EX
mkscript -g deploy -s
.EE
.TP
List matching files under the current tree:
.EX
mkscript -f
.EE
.TP
List matching files only in the current directory:
.EX
mkscript -f 0
.EE
.TP
List matching files up to one subfolder deep:
.EX
mkscript --files 1
.EE
.TP
Look up one or more names and print the resolved location plus its parent directory:
.EX
mkscript -f mkscript README.md
.EE
.TP
Look up a command or filename with a quoted glob pattern:
.EX
mkscript -f 'install-wifi*'
.EE
.TP
Look up newline-separated names from standard input:
.EX
printf "%s\\n" mkscript README.md | mkscript -f
.EE
.TP
Move an existing script and recreate its global shortcut or wrapper if needed:
.EX
mkscript -mv test deploy
.EE
.TP
Create a global shortcut or wrapper:
.EX
mkscript deploy -g
.EE
.TP
Check a global shortcut or wrapper:
.EX
mkscript test -c
.EE
.TP
Remove a global shortcut or wrapper:
.EX
mkscript -r test
.EE
